{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-5476",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly chosen patient is infected with the disease, and let $T$ be the event they tested positive. Then, we require $P(D|T),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(D|T)=\\frac{P(T|D)P(D)}{P(T)}=\\frac{P(T|D)P(D)}{P(T|D)P(D)+P(T|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the probability of an infected person testing positive is $97%,$ and the probability of a non-infected person testing positive is $1%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(T|D)=0.97,\\,P(T|{D}^{′})=0.01.\n$$\n\n- Since only $1%$ of the population has the disease, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.01,\\,P({D}^{′})=1-0.01=0.99.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D|T) & =\\frac{P(T|D)P(D)}{P(T|D)P(D)+P(T|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.97)(0.01)}{(0.97)(0.01)+(0.01)(0.99)} \\\\  & ≈0.49\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
